  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nToday should be drier than compared to the last few days. An\nupper level trof will swing out across western Kansas through\nthe day. In the wake of the trof, there should be synoptic\nsubsidence, leading to a drier forecast. The main exception to\nthis is across the warm/moist sector across south-central\nKansas. There could be a few storms in this airmass and along a\npassing sfc boundary. It should be noted that several CAMs have\nno storms at all across the FA. Otherwise, the rest of the FA\nwill see a dry forecast today. Highs today will be above normal\nwith values in the mid 80s. Lows tonight will continue to be\nmild with values ranging from the 50s in the west to the 60s in\nthe east. \n\nModels continue to indicate that mid level synoptic shortwave\nridging will prevail across western Kansas Sunday. The net\nresult is a continued dry forecast with pops at zero percent. A\nsfc trof axis will be well to our east during the day. The net\nresult is light northeast winds in the wake of this boundary.\nFor highs, we should still continue to see above normal highs\nwith values peaking in the low to mid 80s. Lows will continue to\nremain mild with a mix of 50s to 60s as we head into Monday\nmorning.\n\nThe synoptic pattern begins to change by the beginning of the\nnext business week. Models and their ensemble counterparts are\nforecasting a large upper level trof to begin to carve out\nacross the southwest United States. In addition, there could be\na tropical feature (right now it is Hurricane Polo) that begins\nto head north across Mexico upstream of this synoptic feature. \n\nA slug of monsoonal moisture mixed with moisture from Polo start\nto advect across Kansas by Tuesday. PWATs from both the GFS and\nENS are greater than 1.30\". This is abnormally wet for this time\nof year. With this very moist profile and broad southwesterly\nflow aloft... should help to create an environment that is \nconducive to showers and thunderstorms and potentially heavy \nrainfall. WPC does have much of Kansas in a slight risk on the \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook for Tuesday. There still is some \nspread in where exactly the heavy rainfall axis will be located \nin both the GEFS and ENS. LREF probabilities or rainfall greater\nthan 0.50\" start to increase, with the highest probabilities \nstretching from the Texas panhandle, northwest Oklahoma, and \neastern Kansas throughout the middle portion of the work week.",
